{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12415","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12415","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12415","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rashid","familyName":"Mushkani","name":"Mushkani, Rashid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt Commons: Collective Prompting as Governance for Urban AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:08:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:06:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are entering urban governance, yet their outputs are highly sensitive to prompts that carry value judgments. We propose Prompt Commons - a versioned, community-maintained repository of prompts with governance metadata, licensing, and moderation - to steer model behaviour toward pluralism. Using a Montreal dataset (443 human prompts; 3,317 after augmentation), we pilot three governance states (open, curated, veto-enabled). On a contested policy benchmark, a single-author prompt yields 24 percent neutral outcomes; commons-governed prompts raise neutrality to 48-52 percent while retaining decisiveness where appropriate. In a synthetic incident log, a veto-enabled regime reduces time-to-remediation for harmful outputs from 30.5 +/- 8.9 hours (open) to 5.6 +/- 1.5 hours. We outline licensing (CC BY/BY-SA for prompts with optional OpenRAIL-style restrictions for artefacts), auditable moderation, and safeguards against dominance capture. Prompt governance offers a practical lever for cities to align AI with local values and accountability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12415","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:54Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:39:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12405","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12405","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12405","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen-wai","familyName":"Yim","name":"Yim, Wen-wai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asma Ben","familyName":"Abacha","name":"Abacha, Asma Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Doerning","name":"Doerning, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meliha","familyName":"Yetisgen","name":"Yetisgen, Meliha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MORQA: Benchmarking Evaluation Metrics for Medical Open-Ended Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50 (Primary) 68T45 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:51:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:06:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating natural language generation (NLG) systems in the medical domain presents unique challenges due to the critical demands for accuracy, relevance, and domain-specific expertise. Traditional automatic evaluation metrics, such as BLEU, ROUGE, and BERTScore, often fall short in distinguishing between high-quality outputs, especially given the open-ended nature of medical question answering (QA) tasks where multiple valid responses may exist. In this work, we introduce MORQA (Medical Open-Response QA), a new multilingual benchmark designed to assess the effectiveness of NLG evaluation metrics across three medical visual and text-based QA datasets in English and Chinese. Unlike prior resources, our datasets feature 2-4+ gold-standard answers authored by medical professionals, along with expert human ratings for three English and Chinese subsets. We benchmark both traditional metrics and large language model (LLM)-based evaluators, such as GPT-4 and Gemini, finding that LLM-based approaches significantly outperform traditional metrics in correlating with expert judgments. We further analyze factors driving this improvement, including LLMs' sensitivity to semantic nuances and robustness to variability among reference answers. Our results provide the first comprehensive, multilingual qualitative study of NLG evaluation in the medical domain, highlighting the need for human-aligned evaluation methods. All datasets and annotations will be publicly released to support future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 8 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12405","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:40Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:39:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12395","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12395","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12395","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yash","familyName":"Mundhra","name":"Mundhra, Yash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Valk","name":"Valk, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maliheh","familyName":"Izadi","name":"Izadi, Maliheh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Large Language Models for Functional and Maintainable Code in Industrial Settings: A Case Study at ASML"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:39:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:05:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have shown impressive performance in various domains, including code generation across diverse open-source domains. However, their applicability in proprietary industrial settings, where domain-specific constraints and code interdependencies are prevalent, remains largely unexplored. We present a case study conducted in collaboration with the leveling department at ASML to investigate the performance of LLMs in generating functional, maintainable code within a closed, highly specialized software environment.\n We developed an evaluation framework tailored to ASML's proprietary codebase and introduced a new benchmark. Additionally, we proposed a new evaluation metric, build@k, to assess whether LLM-generated code successfully compiles and integrates within real industrial repositories. We investigate various prompting techniques, compare the performance of generic and code-specific LLMs, and examine the impact of model size on code generation capabilities, using both match-based and execution-based metrics. The findings reveal that prompting techniques and model size have a significant impact on output quality, with few-shot and chain-of-thought prompting yielding the highest build success rates. The difference in performance between the code-specific LLMs and generic LLMs was less pronounced and varied substantially across different model families."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in the 40th IEEE/ACM International Conference on Automated Software Engineering, ASE 2025 (Industry track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12395","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:27Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:39:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12387","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12387","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12387","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed Zayaan","familyName":"S","name":"S, Mohamed Zayaan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Causal-Symbolic Meta-Learning (CSML): Inducing Causal World Models for Few-Shot Generalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:28:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:05:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern deep learning models excel at pattern recognition but remain fundamentally limited by their reliance on spurious correlations, leading to poor generalization and a demand for massive datasets. We argue that a key ingredient for human-like intelligence-robust, sample-efficient learning-stems from an understanding of causal mechanisms. In this work, we introduce Causal-Symbolic Meta-Learning (CSML), a novel framework that learns to infer the latent causal structure of a task distribution. CSML comprises three key modules: a perception module that maps raw inputs to disentangled symbolic representations; a differentiable causal induction module that discovers the underlying causal graph governing these symbols and a graph-based reasoning module that leverages this graph to make predictions. By meta-learning a shared causal world model across a distribution of tasks, CSML can rapidly adapt to novel tasks, including those requiring reasoning about interventions and counterfactuals, from only a handful of examples. We introduce CausalWorld, a new physics-based benchmark designed to test these capabilities. Our experiments show that CSML dramatically outperforms state-of-the-art meta-learning and neuro-symbolic baselines, particularly on tasks demanding true causal inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12387","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:16Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:39:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12381","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12381","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12381","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saajid","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Saajid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesús","familyName":"Pérez-Ríos","name":"Pérez-Ríos, Jesús","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GPU-Accelerated MATLAB Software for Atom-Ion Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Atomic Physics (physics.atom-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Gases (cond-mat.quant-gas)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:19:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:05:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a MATLAB script which can use GPU acceleration to simulate a trapped ion interacting with a low-density cloud of atoms. This script, called atomiongpu.m, can massively parallelize MD simulations of trajectories of a trapped ion and an atom starting far away. The script uses ode45gpu, which is our optimized and specialized implementation of the Runge-Kutta algorithm used in MATLAB's ODE solver ode45. We first discuss the physical system and show how ode45gpu can solve it up to 22x faster than MATLAB's ode45. Then, we show how to easily modify the inputs to atomiongpu.m to account for different kinds of atoms, ions, atom-ion interactions, trap potentials, simulation parameters, initial conditions, and computational hardware, so that atomiongpu.m automatically finds the probability of complex formation, the distribution of observables such as the scattering angle and complex lifetime, and plots of specific trajectories."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12381","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:08Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:39:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12378","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12378","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12378","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruining","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ruining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyuan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Jinhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaidi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Kaidi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Platoon-Centric Green Light Optimal Speed Advisory Using Safe Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:11:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:05:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With recent advancements in Connected Autonomous Vehicles (CAVs), Green Light Optimal Speed Advisory (GLOSA) emerges as a promising eco-driving strategy to reduce the number of stops and idle time at intersections, thereby reducing energy consumption and emissions. Existing studies typically improve energy and travel efficiency for individual CAVs without considering their impacts on the entire mixed-traffic platoon, leading to inefficient traffic flow. While Reinforcement Learning (RL) has the potential to achieve platoon-level control in a mixed-traffic environment, the training of RL is still challenged by (i) car-following safety, i.e., CAVs should not collide with their immediate preceding vehicles, and (ii) red-light safety, i.e., CAVs should not run red lights. To address these challenges, this paper develops a platoon-centric, safe RL-based GLOSA system that uses a multi-agent controller to optimize CAV speed while achieving a balance between energy consumption and travel efficiency. We further incorporate Control Barrier Functions (CBFs) into the RL-based policy to provide explicit safety guarantees in terms of car-following safety and red-light safety. Our simulation results illustrate that our proposed method outperforms state-of-the-art methods in terms of driving safety and platoon energy consumption."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12378","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:04Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:39:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12375","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12375","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12375","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Ripper","name":"Ripper, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ousama","familyName":"Esbel","name":"Esbel, Ousama","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael","familyName":"Fietzek","name":"Fietzek, Rafael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Mühlhäuser","name":"Mühlhäuser, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Kreutz","name":"Kreutz, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffusion-Based Generation and Imputation of Driving Scenarios from Limited Vehicle CAN Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:07:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:05:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training deep learning methods on small time series datasets that also include corrupted samples is challenging. Diffusion models have shown to be effective to generate realistic and synthetic data, and correct corrupted samples through imputation. In this context, this paper focuses on generating synthetic yet realistic samples of automotive time series data. We show that denoising diffusion probabilistic models (DDPMs) can effectively solve this task by applying them to a challenging vehicle CAN-dataset with long-term data and a limited number of samples. Therefore, we propose a hybrid generative approach that combines autoregressive and non-autoregressive techniques. We evaluate our approach with two recently proposed DDPM architectures for time series generation, for which we propose several improvements. To evaluate the generated samples, we propose three metrics that quantify physical correctness and test track adherence. Our best model is able to outperform even the training data in terms of physical correctness, while showing plausible driving behavior. Finally, we use our best model to successfully impute physically implausible regions in the training data, thereby improving the data quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint, Paper has been accepted at ITSC 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12375","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:00Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:39:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Marcuzzo","name":"Marcuzzo, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Zangari","name":"Zangari, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Albarelli","name":"Albarelli, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose","familyName":"Camacho-Collados","name":"Camacho-Collados, Jose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Taher","familyName":"Pilehvar","name":"Pilehvar, Mohammad Taher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MORABLES: A Benchmark for Assessing Abstract Moral Reasoning in LLMs with Fables"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:06:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:04:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As LLMs excel on standard reading comprehension benchmarks, attention is shifting toward evaluating their capacity for complex abstract reasoning and inference. Literature-based benchmarks, with their rich narrative and moral depth, provide a compelling framework for evaluating such deeper comprehension skills. Here, we present MORABLES, a human-verified benchmark built from fables and short stories drawn from historical literature. The main task is structured as multiple-choice questions targeting moral inference, with carefully crafted distractors that challenge models to go beyond shallow, extractive question answering. To further stress-test model robustness, we introduce adversarial variants designed to surface LLM vulnerabilities and shortcuts due to issues such as data contamination. Our findings show that, while larger models outperform smaller ones, they remain susceptible to adversarial manipulation and often rely on superficial patterns rather than true moral reasoning. This brittleness results in significant self-contradiction, with the best models refuting their own answers in roughly 20% of cases depending on the framing of the moral choice. Interestingly, reasoning-enhanced models fail to bridge this gap, suggesting that scale - not reasoning ability - is the primary driver of performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:55Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:38:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12367","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12367","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12367","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Lindmark","name":"Lindmark, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Andersson","name":"Andersson, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenneth","familyName":"Bodin","name":"Bodin, Kenneth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tora","familyName":"Bodin","name":"Bodin, Tora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugo","familyName":"Börjesson","name":"Börjesson, Hugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fredrik","familyName":"Nordfeldth","name":"Nordfeldth, Fredrik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Servin","name":"Servin, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An integrated process for design and control of lunar robotics using AI and simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:02:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:04:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We envision an integrated process for developing lunar construction equipment, where physical design and control are explored in parallel. In this paper, we describe a technical framework that supports this process. It relies on OpenPLX, a readable/writable declarative language that links CAD-models and autonomous systems to high-fidelity, real-time 3D simulations of contacting multibody dynamics, machine regolith interaction forces, and non-ideal sensors. To demonstrate its capabilities, we present two case studies, including an autonomous lunar rover that combines a vision-language model for navigation with a reinforcement learning-based control policy for locomotion."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12367","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:49Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:38:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12350","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12350","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12350","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mayi","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Knowledge Graph Tokenization for Behavior-Aware Generative Next POI Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:25:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:03:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative paradigm, especially powered by Large Language Models (LLMs), has emerged as a new solution to the next point-of-interest (POI) recommendation. Pioneering studies usually adopt a two-stage pipeline, starting with a tokenizer converting POIs into discrete identifiers that can be processed by LLMs, followed by POI behavior prediction tasks to instruction-tune LLM for next POI recommendation. Despite of remarkable progress, they still face two limitations: (1) existing tokenizers struggle to encode heterogeneous signals in the recommendation data, suffering from information loss issue, and (2) previous instruction-tuning tasks only focus on users' POI visit behavior while ignore other behavior types, resulting in insufficient understanding of mobility. To address these limitations, we propose KGTB (Knowledge Graph Tokenization for Behavior-aware generative next POI recommendation). Specifically, KGTB organizes the recommendation data in a knowledge graph (KG) format, of which the structure can seamlessly preserve the heterogeneous information. Then, a KG-based tokenizer is developed to quantize each node into an individual structural ID. This process is supervised by the KG's structure, thus reducing the loss of heterogeneous information. Using generated IDs, KGTB proposes multi-behavior learning that introduces multiple behavior-specific prediction tasks for LLM fine-tuning, e.g., POI, category, and region visit behaviors. Learning on these behavior tasks provides LLMs with comprehensive insights on the target POI visit behavior. Experiments on four real-world city datasets demonstrate the superior performance of KGTB."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12350","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:26Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:38:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12340","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12340","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12340","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolay","familyName":"Banar","name":"Banar, Nikolay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Lotfi","name":"Lotfi, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jens","familyName":"Van Nooten","name":"Van Nooten, Jens","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristina","familyName":"Arhiliuc","name":"Arhiliuc, Cristina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marija","familyName":"Kliocaite","name":"Kliocaite, Marija","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Walter","familyName":"Daelemans","name":"Daelemans, Walter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MTEB-NL and E5-NL: Embedding Benchmark and Models for Dutch"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:08:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:02:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, embedding resources, including models, benchmarks, and datasets, have been widely released to support a variety of languages. However, the Dutch language remains underrepresented, typically comprising only a small fraction of the published multilingual resources. To address this gap and encourage the further development of Dutch embeddings, we introduce new resources for their evaluation and generation. First, we introduce the Massive Text Embedding Benchmark for Dutch (MTEB-NL), which includes both existing Dutch datasets and newly created ones, covering a wide range of tasks. Second, we provide a training dataset compiled from available Dutch retrieval datasets, complemented with synthetic data generated by large language models to expand task coverage beyond retrieval. Finally, we release a series of E5-NL models compact yet efficient embedding models that demonstrate strong performance across multiple tasks. We make our resources publicly available through the Hugging Face Hub and the MTEB package."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12340","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:12Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:38:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12336","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12336","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12336","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adnan Ali","familyName":"Ahmad","name":"Ahmad, Adnan Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoît","familyName":"Commerçon","name":"Commerçon, Benoît","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gilles","familyName":"Chabrier","name":"Chabrier, Gilles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonin","familyName":"Borderies","name":"Borderies, Antonin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Brown Dwarf Formation Through Gravitational Collapse: Insights From 3D Numerical Simulations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:04:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:02:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The formation mechanism of Brown Dwarfs (BDs), whether akin to stars or ejected planetary-mass objects, remains debated. We present the first 3D radiation-MHD simulations of magnetized, turbulent, gravitationally unstable low-mass cores ($0.05-0.1\\ \\mathrm{M_{\\odot}}$) collapsing into proto-BDs. Using the {\\ttfamily RAMSES} code with adaptive mesh refinement, we model the full dynamical range ($10^{5}~-10^{22}\\ \\mathrm{cm^{-3}}$), including radiative transfer (flux limited diffusion) and non-ideal MHD (ambipolar diffusion). Our simulations self-consistently follow the isothermal collapse, first hydrostatic core formation, H$_{2}$ dissociation, and BD birth. The resulting BDs have initial radii $\\approx 0.75\\ \\mathrm{R_{\\odot}}$ and masses $\\approx 0.8\\ \\mathrm{M_{Jup}}$, growing via accretion as we follow the early evolution of the object. Crucially, we find that BDs may form similarly to low-mass stars but with a prolonged first-core phase, supporting a star-like formation scenario."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 7 figures, 1 table. Accepted for publication in ApJL"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12336","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:07Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:38:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12329","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12329","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12329","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengjie Kris","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shengjie Kris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siqin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Siqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncertainty-Aware Hourly Air Temperature Mapping at 2 km Resolution via Physics-Guided Deep Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:01:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:02:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Near-surface air temperature is a key physical property of the Earth's surface. Although weather stations offer continuous monitoring and satellites provide broad spatial coverage, no single data source offers seamless data in a spatiotemporal fashion. Here, we propose a data-driven, physics-guided deep learning approach to generate hourly air temperature data at 2 km resolution over the contiguous United States. The approach, called Amplifier Air-Transformer, first reconstructs GOES-16 surface temperature data obscured by clouds. It does so through a neural network encoded with the annual temperature cycle, incorporating a linear term to amplify ERA5 temperature values at finer scales and convolutional layers to capture spatiotemporal variations. Then, another neural network transforms the reconstructed surface temperature into air temperature by leveraging its latent relationship with key Earth surface properties. The approach is further enhanced with predictive uncertainty estimation through deep ensemble learning to improve reliability. The proposed approach is built and tested on 77.7 billion surface temperature pixels and 155 million air temperature records from weather stations across the contiguous United States (2018-2024), achieving hourly air temperature mapping accuracy of 1.93 C in station-based validation. The proposed approach streamlines surface temperature reconstruction and air temperature prediction, and it can be extended to other satellite sources for seamless air temperature monitoring at high spatiotemporal resolution. The generated data of this study can be downloaded at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15252812, and the project webpage can be found at https://skrisliu.com/HourlyAirTemp2kmUSA/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12329","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:37:57Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:37:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:37:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12287","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12287","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12287","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Nathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cody","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Cody","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Radiographic Disease Detection with MetaCheX, a Context-Aware Multimodal Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T00:44:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing deep learning models for chest radiology often neglect patient metadata, limiting diagnostic accuracy and fairness. To bridge this gap, we introduce MetaCheX, a novel multimodal framework that integrates chest X-ray images with structured patient metadata to replicate clinical decision-making. Our approach combines a convolutional neural network (CNN) backbone with metadata processed by a multilayer perceptron through a shared classifier. Evaluated on the CheXpert Plus dataset, MetaCheX consistently outperformed radiograph-only baseline models across multiple CNN architectures. By integrating metadata, the overall diagnostic accuracy was significantly improved, measured by an increase in AUROC. The results of this study demonstrate that metadata reduces algorithmic bias and enhances model generalizability across diverse patient populations. MetaCheX advances clinical artificial intelligence toward robust, context-aware radiographic disease detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"All authors contributed equally, 5 pages, 2 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12287","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:59Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:37:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:37:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12285","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12285","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12285","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiyong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jiyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deriving the Scaled-Dot-Function via Maximum Likelihood Estimation and Maximum Entropy Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T19:52:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we present a maximum likelihood estimation approach to determine the value vector in transformer models. We model the sequence of value vectors, key vectors, and the query vector as a sequence of Gaussian distributions. The variance in each Gaussian distribution depends on the time step, the corresponding key vector, and the query vector. The mean value in each Gaussian distribution depends on the time step, and the corresponding value vector. This analysis may offer a new explanation of the scaled-dot-product function or softmax function used in transformer architectures [1]. Another explanation, inspired by [4], is based on the maximum entropy approach in natural language processing [5]. In this approach, a query vector and key vectors are used to derive the feature functions for the maximum entropy model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12285","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:56Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:36:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12283","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12283","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12283","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ana Elisa","familyName":"Lopez-Miranda","name":"Lopez-Miranda, Ana Elisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiffany","familyName":"Timbers","name":"Timbers, Tiffany","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rohan","familyName":"Alexander","name":"Alexander, Rohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompting the Professoriate: A Qualitative Study of Instructor Perspectives on LLMs in Data Science Education"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T17:32:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have shifted in just a few years from novelty to ubiquity, raising fundamental questions for data science education. Tasks once used to teach coding, writing, and problem-solving can now be completed by LLMs, forcing educators to reconsider both pedagogy and assessment. To understand how instructors are adapting, we conducted semi-structured interviews with 42 instructors from 33 institutions in 10 countries in June and July 2025. Our qualitative analysis reveals a pragmatic mix of optimism and concern. Many respondents view LLMs as inevitable classroom tools -- comparable to calculators or Wikipedia -- while others worry about de-skilling, misplaced confidence, and uneven integration across institutions. Around 58 per cent have already introduced demonstrations, guided activities, or make extensive use of LLMs in their courses, though most expect change to remain slow and uneven. That said, 31 per cent have not used LLMs to teach students and do not plan to. We highlight some instructional innovations, including AI-aware assessments, reflective use of LLMs as tutors, and course-specific chatbots. By sharing these perspectives, we aim to help data science educators adapt collectively to ensure curricula keep pace with technological change."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"45 pages, 16 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12283","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:54Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:36:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12278","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12278","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12278","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanru","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Wanru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibin","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Zhibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinsong","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jinsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PATIMT-Bench: A Multi-Scenario Benchmark for Position-Aware Text Image Machine Translation in Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T08:33:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text Image Machine Translation (TIMT) aims to translate texts embedded within an image into another language. Current TIMT studies primarily focus on providing translations for all the text within an image, while neglecting to provide bounding boxes and covering limited scenarios. In this work, we extend traditional TIMT into position-aware TIMT (PATIMT), aiming to support fine-grained and layoutpreserving translation, which holds great practical value but remains largely unexplored. This task comprises two key sub-tasks: regionspecific translation and full-image translation with grounding. To support existing models on PATIMT and conduct fair evaluation, we construct the PATIMT benchmark (PATIMTBench), which consists of 10 diverse real-world scenarios. Specifically, we introduce an Adaptive Image OCR Refinement Pipeline, which adaptively selects appropriate OCR tools based on scenario and refines the results of text-rich images. To ensure evaluation reliability, we further construct a test set, which contains 1,200 high-quality instances manually annotated and reviewed by human experts. After fine-tuning on our data, compact Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) achieve state-of-the-art performance on both sub-tasks. Experimental results also highlight the scalability and generalizability of our training data"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12278","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:47Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:36:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iman Mohamed","familyName":"Attia","name":"Attia, Iman Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"New generalized unit distributions based on order statistics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T07:47:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the present paper, the author discusses the derivation of unit distributions and the derivation of the generalized form using the order statistics. The author discusses the Kumaraswamy as the smallest order statistic of the unit power distribution derived from the inverse Weibull distribution. The author discusses the unit Rayleigh distribution and how it can be generalized using the smallest, largest, and kth order statistics. Using the order statistics to generalize a distribution differs from other techniques like the power transformation and T-X family (transformed-transformer) method. For the discussed distribution, the author demonstrates the basic functions and properties with real data analysis."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:44Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:36:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12273","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12273","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12273","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangqi","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Liangqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong-Jun","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Dong-Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher G.","familyName":"Brinton","name":"Brinton, Christopher G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sabine","familyName":"Brunswicker","name":"Brunswicker, Sabine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMAP: LLM-Assisted Multi-Objective Route Planning with User Preferences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T02:30:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rise of large language models (LLMs) has made natural language-driven route planning an emerging research area that encompasses rich user objectives. Current research exhibits two distinct approaches: direct route planning using LLM-as-Agent and graph-based searching strategies. However, LLMs in the former approach struggle to handle extensive map data, while the latter shows limited capability in understanding natural language preferences. Additionally, a more critical challenge arises from the highly heterogeneous and unpredictable spatio-temporal distribution of users across the globe. In this paper, we introduce a novel LLM-Assisted route Planning (LLMAP) system that employs an LLM-as-Parser to comprehend natural language, identify tasks, and extract user preferences and recognize task dependencies, coupled with a Multi-Step Graph construction with iterative Search (MSGS) algorithm as the underlying solver for optimal route finding. Our multi-objective optimization approach adaptively tunes objective weights to maximize points of interest (POI) quality and task completion rate while minimizing route distance, subject to three key constraints: user time limits, POI opening hours, and task dependencies. We conduct extensive experiments using 1,000 routing prompts sampled with varying complexity across 14 countries and 27 cities worldwide. The results demonstrate that our approach achieves superior performance with guarantees across multiple constraints."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12273","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:40Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:36:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12269","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12269","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12269","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinmeiyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinmeiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Research on Short-Video Platform User Decision-Making via Multimodal Temporal Modeling and Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T16:28:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper proposes the MT-DQN model, which integrates a Transformer, Temporal Graph Neural Network (TGNN), and Deep Q-Network (DQN) to address the challenges of predicting user behavior and optimizing recommendation strategies in short-video environments. Experiments demonstrated that MT-DQN consistently outperforms traditional concatenated models, such as Concat-Modal, achieving an average F1-score improvement of 10.97% and an average NDCG@5 improvement of 8.3%. Compared to the classic reinforcement learning model Vanilla-DQN, MT-DQN reduces MSE by 34.8% and MAE by 26.5%. Nonetheless, we also recognize challenges in deploying MT-DQN in real-world scenarios, such as its computational cost and latency sensitivity during online inference, which will be addressed through future architectural optimization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12269","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:35Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:36:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12259","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12259","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12259","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenneth G.","familyName":"Young","name":"Young, Kenneth G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum-Inspired Stacked Integrated Concept Graph Model (QISICGM) for Diabetes Risk Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T18:26:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Quantum-Inspired Stacked Integrated Concept Graph Model (QISICGM) is an innovative machine learning framework that harnesses quantum-inspired techniques to predict diabetes risk with exceptional accuracy and efficiency. Utilizing the PIMA Indians Diabetes dataset augmented with 2,000 synthetic samples to mitigate class imbalance (total: 2,768 samples, 1,949 positives), QISICGM integrates a self-improving concept graph with a stacked ensemble comprising Random Forests (RF), Extra Trees (ET), transformers, convolutional neural networks (CNNs), and feed-forward neural networks (FFNNs). This approach achieves an out-of-fold (OOF) F1 score of 0.8933 and an AUC of 0.8699, outperforming traditional methods. Quantum inspired elements, such as phase feature mapping and neighborhood sequence modeling, enrich feature representations, enabling CPU-efficient inference at 8.5 rows per second. This paper presents a detailed architecture, theoretical foundations, code insights, and performance evaluations, including visualizations from the outputs subfolder. The open-source implementation (v1.0.0) is available at https://github.com/keninayoung/QISICGM, positioning QISICGM as a potential benchmark for AI-assisted clinical triage in diabetes and beyond. Ultimately, this work emphasizes trustworthy AI through calibration, interpretability, and open-source reproducibility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 3 figures, includes performance tables and visualizations. Proposes a Quantum-Inspired Stacked Integrated Concept Graph Model (QISICGM) that integrates phase feature mapping, self-improving concept graphs, and neighborhood sequence modeling within a stacked ensemble. Demonstrates improved F1 and AUC on an augmented PIMA Diabetes dataset with efficient CPU inference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12259","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:21Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:36:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12251","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12251","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12251","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duong Q.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Duong Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quy P.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Quy P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nguyen","familyName":"Van Nhon","name":"Van Nhon, Nguyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quang-Thinh","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Quang-Thinh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Nguyen-Xuan","name":"Nguyen-Xuan, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"V-Math: An Agentic Approach to the Vietnamese National High School Graduation Mathematics Exams"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T07:22:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper develops an autonomous agentic framework called V-Math that aims to assist Vietnamese high school students in preparing for the National High School Graduation Mathematics Exams (NHSGMEs). The salient framework integrates three specialized AI agents: a specification-matrix-conditioned question generator, a solver/explainer for detailed step-by-step reasoning, and a personalized tutor that adapts to student performance. Beyond enabling self-paced student practice, V-Math supports teachers by generating innovative, compliant exam questions and building diverse, high-quality question banks. This reduces manual workload and enriches instructional resources. We describe the system architecture, focusing on practice modes for learners and teacher-oriented features for question generation. Preliminary evaluations demonstrate that V-Math produces matrix-aligned exams with high solution accuracy, delivers coherent explanations, and enhances the variety of practice materials. These results highlight its potential to support scalable, equitable mathematics preparation aligned with national standards while also empowering teachers through AI-assisted exam creation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12251","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:10Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:36:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12243","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12243","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12243","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murray","familyName":"Cutforth","name":"Cutforth, Murray","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiffany","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Tiffany","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Zahtila","name":"Zahtila, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alireza","familyName":"Doostan","name":"Doostan, Alireza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Darve","name":"Darve, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bi-fidelity Interpolative Decomposition for Multimodal Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:38:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:00:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-fidelity simulation is a widely used strategy to reduce the computational cost of many-query numerical simulation tasks such as uncertainty quantification, design space exploration, and design optimization. The reduced basis approach based on bi-fidelity interpolative decomposition is one such approach, which identifies a reduced basis, along with an interpolation rule in that basis, from low-fidelity samples to approximate the corresponding high-fidelity samples. However, as illustrated in the present study, when the model response is multi-modal and mode occupancy is stochastic, the assumptions underpinning this approach may not hold, thus leading to inaccurate estimates. We introduce the multi-modal interpolative decomposition method using bi-fidelity data, an extension tailored for this use case. Our work is motivated by a complex engineering application: a laser-ignited methane-oxygen rocket combustor evaluated over uncertain input parameters, exhibiting a bifurcation-like phenomenon in some regions of parameter space. Unlike the standard bi-fidelity interpolative decomposition approach, the proposed method can approximate a dataset of high-fidelity simulations for 16\\% of the cost, while maintaining relatively high correlation (0.70--0.90) with parameter sensitivities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12243","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:35:59Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:36:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12239","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12239","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12239","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanyam","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Sanyam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khuram","familyName":"Naveed","name":"Naveed, Khuram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Illia","familyName":"Oleksiienko","name":"Oleksiienko, Illia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandros","familyName":"Iosifidis","name":"Iosifidis, Alexandros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruben","familyName":"Pauwels","name":"Pauwels, Ruben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InJecteD: Analyzing Trajectories and Drift Dynamics in Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models for 2D Point Cloud Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T14:53:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:00:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work introduces InJecteD, a framework for interpreting Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPMs) by analyzing sample trajectories during the denoising process of 2D point cloud generation. We apply this framework to three datasets from the Datasaurus Dozen bullseye, dino, and circle using a simplified DDPM architecture with customizable input and time embeddings. Our approach quantifies trajectory properties, including displacement, velocity, clustering, and drift field dynamics, using statistical metrics such as Wasserstein distance and cosine similarity. By enhancing model transparency, InJecteD supports human AI collaboration by enabling practitioners to debug and refine generative models. Experiments reveal distinct denoising phases: initial noise exploration, rapid shape formation, and final refinement, with dataset-specific behaviors example, bullseyes concentric convergence vs. dinos complex contour formation. We evaluate four model configurations, varying embeddings and noise schedules, demonstrating that Fourier based embeddings improve trajectory stability and reconstruction quality"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12239","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:35:54Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:35:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:35:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12234","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12234","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12234","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Burns","name":"Burns, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Douglas W.","familyName":"Scharre","name":"Scharre, Douglas W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xia","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Flexible Multimodal Neuroimaging Fusion for Alzheimer's Disease Progression Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T16:59:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:00:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disease with high inter-patient variance in rate of cognitive decline. AD progression prediction aims to forecast patient cognitive decline and benefits from incorporating multiple neuroimaging modalities. However, existing multimodal models fail to make accurate predictions when many modalities are missing during inference, as is often the case in clinical settings. To increase multimodal model flexibility under high modality missingness, we introduce PerM-MoE, a novel sparse mixture-of-experts method that uses independent routers for each modality in place of the conventional, single router. Using T1-weighted MRI, FLAIR, amyloid beta PET, and tau PET neuroimaging data from the Alzheimer's Disease Neuroimaging Initiative (ADNI), we evaluate PerM-MoE, state-of-the-art Flex-MoE, and unimodal neuroimaging models on predicting two-year change in Clinical Dementia Rating-Sum of Boxes (CDR-SB) scores under varying levels of modality missingness. PerM-MoE outperforms the state of the art in most variations of modality missingness and demonstrates more effective utility of experts than Flex-MoE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at Applications of Medical AI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12234","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:35:47Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:35:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:35:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":281,"totalPages":12,"page":11},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=11\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=12\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29"}}